Switching from Docker/Conda to Singularity (now often called Apptainer) is the right move for HPC environments. Singularity is designed to run with the same permissions as your user, and it handles GPU/OpenGL much more gracefully than Docker.

To run a GUI application with OpenGL, you need two things: Hardware Acceleration (the --nv or --rocm flag) and X11 Socket Binding.

1. The Singularity Definition File (starviewer.def)
Since you need Qt, NetCDF, and C++, use this definition file. It bootstraps from Ubuntu but installs all the dependencies you need.

Bash
Bootstrap: docker
From: ubuntu:22.04

%post
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update && apt-get install -y \
        build-essential cmake git \
        qtbase5-dev qt5-qmake libqt5gui5 libqt5widgets5 \
        libnetcdf-dev libnetcdf-c++4-dev \
        libgl1-mesa-dri libgl1-mesa-glx libglu1-mesa \
        libxcb-xinerama0 x11-apps

%environment
    # Crucial for finding libraries inside the container
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
    # Force software rendering if hardware fails, or comment out for GPU
    # export LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1

%runscript
    exec ./starviewer "$@"
2. Build the Image
You usually need sudo or --fakeroot to build a Singularity image:

Bash
singularity build starviewer.sif starviewer.def
3. How to Run with OpenGL Hardware Acceleration
This is where the magic happens. Singularity has a built-in flag that pulls the host's NVIDIA drivers into the container automatically.

For NVIDIA GPUs:

Bash
singularity exec --nv starviewer.sif ./starviewer
For AMD GPUs:

Bash
singularity exec --rocm starviewer.sif ./starviewer
Why this is better than Conda: The --nv flag ensures the libGL.so inside the container matches the kernel driver on the host node (u22g03).

4. Running it in your tst.sh
Update your script to use the container. Singularity automatically mounts your home directory and /scratch, so your paths should still work.

Bash
#!/bin/bash
# tst.sh inside/with Singularity

# 1. Set your display (if not already set by SSH)
export DISPLAY=$DISPLAY

# 2. Run the app through Singularity
# --nv: Links host NVIDIA drivers
# --bind: Manually map specific scratch paths if they aren't auto-mounted
singularity exec --nv \
    --bind /scratch4/NAGAPE/epic/Wei.Huang \
    starviewer.sif \
    /scratch4/NAGAPE/epic/Wei.Huang/src/nv/starviewer/build/main/starviewer -mpi mpi
Troubleshooting OpenGL in Singularity
If you still get the QXcbIntegration or GLX errors:

The "No-Shared-Memory" Fix: export QT_X11_NO_MITSHM=1 (Add this to your %environment section in the .def file).

Verify Drivers:
Run singularity exec --nv starviewer.sif nvidia-smi. If this fails, the --nv flag isn't finding your host drivers.

Check Display:
Run singularity exec starviewer.sif xclock. If a clock doesn't appear on your screen, your X11 forwarding is broken, regardless of OpenGL.

Are you running this on a node that definitely has a physical GPU, or are you trying to get hardware acceleration over a standard CPU-only login node?

